// S~S  SimpleSal signal starts  S~S

// [ARM Function Pointers.note]         search in ssTEA source files for a reference to this file.
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Function validation looks closely at the function pointer to make sure it is reasonable:
   from the same area of memory as this code, for example.  Otherwise the value can't be validated
   because the only knowledge of a valid address is in the compiler and linker, not the source code.
   ThumbBit is only checked to show that the 32-bit address has 2 Least Significant Bits "borrowed".
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
   #define ThumbBit (Bit0)
//
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   ssTEA does not in normal processing actually care about the value of the pointer to code,
   but ssTEA will not run code at location of true Null (believed to be 0x0).  Often, the
   interrupt vector table is at location 0x0, and/or, the "data" there is processor code.
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   ssTEA will happily run any other code that is pointed at, any memory location.  This
   may result in HARD FAULTs in ARM processors if the software gives ssTEA a bad pointer.
   It is also true that for a pointer to ARM Thumb code (indicated by bit 0 and 1 being used
   by ARM in a 32-bit code address), this code will happily call Thumb code at location 0x3,
   and the odds are pretty good the processor instruction there is NOT a thumb instruction.
   Going deeper in the validation of a function pointer requires build-specific knowledge.
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   if (ssE_pAboutEv->pEvFunc == pEvFuncNull)
//

// S~S  SimpleSal signal ends  S~S
